In article <3101@wpi.wpi.edu> cmoir@wpi.wpi.edu (C-More) writes:
>In article <3631@cps3xx.UUCP> hendrick@frith.UUCP (Hendrickson) writes:
>>How do you steal using the dog?  (We have one; so far it's only been
>>good for killing pesky monsters.)
>
>SPOILERS AHEAD...

>What you do is walk one step into the shop, pushing the dog in front
>of you.  Then you drop a tripe ration on that square, and step back
>into the doorway.  Let the dog come eat the tripe ration.  When you do
>this, your dog is 'trained' to retrieve items out of the shop and
>leave them on *that* square.  This is important, as all items found on
>that first square are *free*!  So your dog clears out the shop, and
>the shopkeeper is none the wiser.  An added effect to this: The dog
>will NOT pick up cursed items...so anything he gives you is curse
>free!
>
>Remember though...when you are the Hand of Elbereth, this won't work,
>as the shopkeeper will stay put in the doorway.

This is true as far as it goes, but is not the whole story.  Indeed,
the dog becomes trained by eating tripe rations, but the effect varies
depending on several things:

	1) How long it has been since the dog dropped any item.

	2) The distance between to dog and you (I forget if the distance
	   is measured when he dropped the item, or when he ate the
	   ration, but it's one of those)

So my procedure is to wait at a convenient spot, with blank squares all
around, until the dog gets next to me and moves away leaving an item
behind.  If I'm still lined up with him, I throw the ration at him.
It always misses, and he almost always eats it.  He then becomes
a frantic pack-rat for a while, and really enjoys fetching until the
training starts to wear thin.  If I can, I keep reinforcing with more
tripe rations.

However, since I learned the joys of piety, I hardly ever do this any
more.  And the gods are indeed good.  I also hardly ever eat anything
except royal jelly.


